few of any prominent nicaraguan  diplomats have dared turn on president  daniel ortega until now  in the plenary session of the  organization of american states  nicaragua's ambassador arturo mcfeels  has to speak in the name of more than  117 political prisoners and 350 people  killed by his government  denouncing the dictatorship of my  country is not easy but to continue  remaining silent and defending the  indefensible is impossible  i have to speak out even if i am afraid  even if my future and my families are  uncertain i have to speak out  arturo mcfeels has been a  washington-based diplomat for the  government for a decade his surprise  decision to denounce ortega came days  after former presidential candidate  cristiana chamorro was sentenced to  eight years in detention for financial  crimes and so-called ideological  deviations  48 other opposition figures who've been  tried inside managua's notorious el  chipote prison have been sentenced to  between 8 and 13 years in prison on what  are widely described as politically  motivated and trumped-up charges  ambassador mcfeel says disenchantment  with ortega is widespread but that  public servants are afraid to speak out  days before our withdrawal from the oas  we had a virtual meeting in the foreign  ministry with a team of presidential  advisors i suggested they release at  least 20 elderly political prisoners and  20 other ordinary prisoners for health  reasons i told them that it would be a  humane and politically intelligent  action since no one should die in prison  especially if they are innocent and lack  adequate medical care they want me to  shut up  last month hugo torres who saved  president daniel ortega's life when they  both fought to overthrow a former  nicaraguan dictatorship died in prison  and vilma nunes tells al jazeera that  others are in critical condition  all the prisoners are suffering from  health issues for example former deputy  foreign minister victor hugo tanoco is  in a grave 